SUMMARY:EASC Roundtable: Xiao Hai and Tony Hao
DESCRIPTION:Xiao Hai was born in Shangqiu City in Henan Province in China
 . At fifteen he moved to Shenzhen to join the workforce\, moving between 
 cities and factory jobs while writing poetry in his spare time. He is the
  author of the poetry collection Sisyphus on the Wenyu River\, and transl
 ations of his poetry have appeared in World Literature Today and Chinese 
 Literature Today.Tony Hao is a New Haven\, Connecticut-based Chinese-to-E
 nglish literary translator and writer. Authors he has translated include 
 Taiwanese novelist Tong Wei-Ger 童偉格\, Chinese worker-poet Xiao Hai 
 小海\, Taiwanese transgender writer Liu Chen-Chun 劉宸君\, and Chine
 se short story writer Ban Yu 班宇.  His debut book-length translation\,
  Adrift in the South\, a memoir by Xiao Hai\, was published by Granta Mag
 azine Editions in May\, 2026.  His short-form translations have appeared 
 in Granta\, The Wall Street Journal\, The Georgia Review\, The Offing\, B
 ooks from Taiwan\, Florescence (The Taipei Chinese PEN)\, MAYDAY Magazine
 \, and elsewhere. His work has been recognized by the PEN Translates Gran
 t\, the Granum Foundation Translation Prize\, Art Omi Writers: Translatio
 n Lab\, the Vermont Studio Center\, BCLT's Advanced Translation Workshop\
 , the Bread Loaf Translators' Conference\, and elsewhere.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Xiao Hai</strong> was born in Sha
 ngqiu City in Henan Province in China. At fifteen he moved to Shenzhen to
  join the workforce\, moving between cities and factory jobs while writin
 g poetry in his spare time. He is the author of the poetry collection <i>
 Sisyphus on the Wenyu River</i>\, and translations of his poetry have app
 eared in <i>World Literature Today</i> and <i>Chinese Literature Today</i
 >.</p><p class="" data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><strong>Tony Hao</strong
 > is a New Haven\, Connecticut-based Chinese-to-English literary translat
 or and writer. Authors he has translated include Taiwanese novelist Tong 
 Wei-Ger 童偉格\, Chinese worker-poet Xiao Hai 小海\, Taiwanese trans
 gender writer Liu Chen-Chun 劉宸君\, and Chinese short story writer Ba
 n Yu 班宇.</p><p class="" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">  His debut bo
 ok-length translation\, <em>Adrift in the South</em>\, a memoir by Xiao H
 ai\, was published by Granta Magazine Editions in May\, 2026.</p><p class
 ="" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">  His short-form translations have app
 eared in <em>Granta</em>\, <em>The Wall Street Journal\, The Georgia Revi
 ew\, The Offing</em>\, <em>Books from Taiwan\, Florescence (The Taipei Ch
 inese PEN)\, MAYDAY Magazine</em>\, and elsewhere. His work has been reco
 gnized by the PEN Translates Grant\, the Granum Foundation Translation Pr
 ize\, Art Omi Writers: Translation Lab\, the Vermont Studio Center\, BCLT
 's Advanced Translation Workshop\, the Bread Loaf Translators' Conference
 \, and elsewhere.</p>
